# pacman scriptlet for the `voice` package.
#
# The udev rule tags input event devices with `uaccess` so the logged-in seat
# user can read keyboard events for push-to-talk. udev does not pick a new rule
# up on its own, hence the reload here and after removal.
#
# Nothing here may claim something the build did not do: this file ships in both
# the GPU and the CPU-only package, so anything that differs between them is
# decided at runtime from what actually landed on disk.

_reload_udev() {
    if command -v udevadm >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        udevadm control --reload-rules >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
        udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=input >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    fi
}

_python_minor() {
    /usr/bin/python3 -c 'import sys; print("%d.%d" % sys.version_info[:2])' 2>/dev/null \
        || echo "its build version"
}

post_install() {
    _reload_udev
    cat <<'MSG'
voice is installed. Three things to do, once:

  1. Assign the push-to-talk key. Start the daemon (`voice daemon`, or log out
     and back in - it autostarts), then open
       Settings -> Keyboard -> Keyboard Shortcuts
     and give "voice" a key. The desktop owns that key from then on; the
     hotkeys.portal_* settings are only a first-run preference.
  2. Run `voice doctor`. It says what works on this machine and what to fix.
  3. The first dictation opens the desktop's RemoteDesktop permission dialog -
     accept it. The restore token is cached, so it asks once.

The local backend downloads its model on first use (about 1.6 GB, cached under
~/.cache/huggingface).
MSG

    # Which build is this? Ask the filesystem, not the packager.
    if [ -d /usr/lib/voice/cuda ]; then
        cat <<'MSG'

This build bundles the CUDA 12 runtime, so transcription uses your NVIDIA GPU as
soon as the driver is there - `voice doctor`'s cuda line says whether it found
one. With no usable GPU it falls back to CPU int8 by itself.
MSG
    else
        cat <<'MSG'

This is the CPU-only build: no CUDA runtime is bundled, and transcription runs
on CPU int8. That is a few times faster than real time on an ordinary machine,
and `voice doctor`'s cuda line will say there is no CUDA device - which is
expected here, and does not fail the run. For GPU transcription, rebuild with
`cd packaging && makepkg -si` (adds roughly 3 GB).
MSG
    fi

    printf '\n'
    printf '%s\n' \
"One thing to know about updates: this package bundles Python extension modules" \
"built for python $(_python_minor), and depends on exactly that minor version." \
"When Arch moves on, pacman stops and names voice as what is in the way, rather" \
"than letting it break silently with an ImportError nothing warned you about." \
"Rebuilding clears it and the python upgrade goes through:" \
"  cd packaging && makepkg -si"

    cat <<'MSG'

Autostart comes from /etc/xdg/autostart/io.github.vampyren.voice.desktop. To
stop it for your user without touching the package:
  mkdir -p ~/.config/autostart
  cp /etc/xdg/autostart/io.github.vampyren.voice.desktop ~/.config/autostart/
  echo 'Hidden=true' >> ~/.config/autostart/io.github.vampyren.voice.desktop

If you also installed from a git clone, ~/.local/bin/voice shadows /usr/bin/voice
on most PATHs; run ./install.sh --uninstall in the checkout to clear it.
MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
    _reload_udev
}

post_remove() {
    _reload_udev
    cat <<'MSG'
voice is removed. Every file the package owned is gone. These are yours, so
they were left alone - here is exactly what remains and how to clear it.

Your settings and history:
  rm -rf ~/.config/voice          # config.toml - your key, language, profiles
  rm -rf ~/.local/state/voice     # dictation history, the portal permission token

An autostart override, only if you made one by hand:
  rm -f ~/.config/autostart/io.github.vampyren.voice.desktop

The downloaded speech models - roughly 1.6 GB, and SHARED with any other tool
that uses Hugging Face. Deleting it will make those re-download:
  rm -rf ~/.cache/huggingface/hub/models--Systran--faster-whisper-*
  # only if nothing else on this machine uses Hugging Face:
  # rm -rf ~/.cache/huggingface

A daemon that was already running keeps going until you log out. To stop it now:
  pkill -f 'voice daemon'
MSG
}
